A husband and wife play a card game with the following rules:
a. Whenever possible, the second player in each round must follow the suit led by the first player, while the first player may lead any suit;
b. The winner of each round becomes the first player for the next round.
Given:
(1) Each player holds four cards, with suit distributions as follows: Husband: Spade—Spade—Heart—Club; Wife: Diamond—Diamond—Heart—Spade;
(2) Each player was the first player twice;
(3) Each player won two rounds;
(4) In each round, the suit led by the first player was different;
(5) In each round, two different suits were played.
Note: The trump is any card of a certain suit, which can: (1) be played when the player has no card of the led suit—thus, a trump card beats any card of the other three suits; (2) be led like a card of any other suit. The trump won at least one round.

Based on the following conditions, infer each child's name, illness, pajama color, and comfort item received:
(1) The child wearing red pajamas receives a book;
(2) The child with measles (who is neither Bailey nor Frank) receives a toy;
(3) Alice has mumps, and another child (wearing green pajamas) is visited by a friend;
(4) Frank wears orange pajamas and does not have tonsillitis;
(5) Li Yi has scarlet fever and his pajamas are not green;
(6) The child with chickenpox does not receive ice cream;
(7) The one wearing blue pajamas is neither Robin nor Li Yi;
(8) There is a child wearing yellow pajamas;
(9) There is a child who receives jelly.

Given four numbers A, B, C, and D, they satisfy the following conditions:
(1) The sum of A and B is greater than the sum of C and D;
(2) The sum of A and D is greater than the sum of B and C;
(3) The sum of B and D is greater than the sum of A and C.
